WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.780
Welcome to Academy
Dialogues It Starts With Us.

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.400
My name is Shawn Finnie.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.540
We are continuing our
series of conversations

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.660
about race, ethnicity,
history, opportunity,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.960
in the art of filmmaking
hosted by the Academy.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.750
Today's topic is
owning our stories.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.450
We're incredibly honored to
have Bird Runningwater, director

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.120
of Sundance Institute's
Indigenous Programs

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.430
moderating our
conversation today.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:27.300
Joining Bird, are screenwriter
Misan Sagay, writer, director,

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.910
actor Elle-Máijá
Tailfeathers, writer,

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.510
producer Ligiah Villalobos
and writer, director,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.760
producer Lulu Wang.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.100
Here's a little background
on each of our speakers.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.770
As director of the Sundance
Institute's Indigenous

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:44.370
Programs, Bird oversees the
Sundance Native filmmakers lab,

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:47.940
the Native producers fellowship
and the festival's Native form.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.090
A native of the Cheyenne
Mescalero Apache tribes,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.550
Bird has been with Sundance
for the last 10 years,

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.070
supporting the buildup of
a global Indigenous film

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.000
community.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.430
He is skilled in leading panel
discussions and workshops

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.370
around the world and has
worked with major institutions,

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.250
including the Ford and
Rockefeller foundations.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.650
He holds degrees in
journalism, public affairs,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.160
and Native studies.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.890
In fact, he was
the first graduate

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.660
of the Native American
Studies Program

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.150
at the University of Oklahoma.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.090
Misan is a native of Nigeria,
who was raised in England

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.730
and amazingly had a career
as an emergency room doctor

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.800
before becoming a screenwriter.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.130
She wrote the script for
the 2013 movie Belle,

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.500
about the daughter of an
enslaved African woman

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.730
and a British admiral set
in 1700s British society.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.400
Her credits also include the
teleplay for the Oprah Winfrey

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.530
produced movie, Their
Eyes Were Watching

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.080
God, and co-writer for
the six-part series,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.980
Gorilla, starring Idris Elba
and produced by John Ridley,

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.640
the writer of 12 Years a Slave.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.810
Elle-Maija Tailfeathers
is a rising filmmaker

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.090
who is bringing compelling
authentic stories

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.400
about Indigenous
people to the screen.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.770
She comes from Sámi,
the Indigenous territory

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.080
of the Sámi people
in northern Europe.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.720
She is also part of the
Kainai First Nation Blackfoot

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.920
Confederacy.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.800
Her films include the multi
award, a documentary short,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.039
Rebel, which is autobiographical
and the feature, The Body

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.250
Remembers When The World Broke
Open, which she co-wrote,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.710
co-directed, and acted in.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.590
It premiered in 2019 at
the Toronto Film Festival,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.440
receiving three awards,
including best picture

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.100
and at the Berlin Film Festival.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.500
Ligiah Villalobos
has had a long career

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.240
as both studio executive
and a filmmaker.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.450
Her many film credits
include writing and executive

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.240
producing the independent
feature, La Misma Luna,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.250
also known as Under
the Same Moon.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.350
Writing the film
Firelight, produced

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.290
by Alicia Keys and Mary
Martin and consulting on Pixar

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.630
animated features
including Coco.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.810
Firelight was nominated
for NAACP Image Award

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.410
and received a 2013
humanities prize.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.780
Lulu Wang first gained
international attention

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.200
for writing and directing
Posthumous in 2014.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.260
And then again in 2019
for her second feature,

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.220
The Farewell, which she wrote,
directed, and co-produced.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.530
The Farewell follows struggling
New York City artist,

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.500
Billi, played by Awkwafina,
who travels to China

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.110
to visit her dying grandmother.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.110
Wang filmed The Farewell
in the neighborhood

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.110
where her own grandmother
actually lived.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:15.710
And she cast her great aunt, Lu
Hung, as herself in the film.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:19.010
Last year variety named Wang
one of 10 directors to watch

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.920
and Awkwafina won a Golden
Globe for her performance.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.320
The Farewell also won the
Independent Spirit award

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.850
for best film.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.100
We look forward to hearing
your conversation everyone.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.700
Bird, take it away.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:30.287
Thank you.

00:03:30.287 --> 00:03:32.120
And I want to thank
everyone for joining us,

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.290
especially our filmmakers,
our screenwriters.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.890
I feel so honored to be here
having a conversation with you.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:43.190
I myself am just
logging in essentially

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:47.180
from Gabrielino-Tongva
lands and Tataviam

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.580
lands, which Hollywood
is built upon.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.710
I don't think that's a
fact that's very well known

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:53.810
within many communities.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.550
But I'm very honored to
come here to you today.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.390
But I also have to
mention that, I myself

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.210
descend from matrilineal and
matriarchal Indigenous nations.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:04.880
And so I feel very
humbled myself

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.820
to be speaking with such
four powerful women,

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:08.930
such as yourselves.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:12.920
And I think the great voices
that you bring to your work,

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:16.140
and to our screens, and to
audiences around the world.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.019
So I just want to first
of all say, thank you

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:20.269
for what you give
and what you do.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:23.900
I wanted to just basically
start our conversation

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.140
by I guess asking you--

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.547
I think, as a point of
view on our conversation.

00:04:29.547 --> 00:04:31.130
I wonder if somebody
could kick us off

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:35.120
just by telling us how they
would describe what they've

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.670
defined as their
own authentic voice

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.260
that they're lending
to their work.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.640
Does somebody want to
kick us off with that?

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.160
Lulu, do you want to start?

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.290
Sure.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:46.220
Yeah.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.210
I think it took me
a really long time.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.440
I think I'm still in
the process of discovery

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.380
of what that means
to have a voice

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.650
and to have a
voice in your work.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:04.220
So for me-- for a long time it
was finding the juxtaposition

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.910
of pathos and humor,
which for me is

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:15.620
very much at the heart of my
family, my culture, the way

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.320
that I see the world, the
way that my family has

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.540
taught me to see the world.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:25.670
So humor was a big part of--

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:28.970
like finding the kind
of humor in my work

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.880
that I wanted to
show was a big part

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:36.200
of my process of it landing.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.290
Because humor is so specific
to every culture and it

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:41.450
may not be easily translatable.

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:45.260
So that was a really difficult
thing when I set out especially

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:47.600
to make The Farewell, to
say, oh, this is funny.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.620
And if someone goes,
well is it funny?

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.960
And how do you break
that down, right?

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.450
Because what someone
finds funny it just is.

00:05:56.450 --> 00:06:02.120
And so that was
a big exploration

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:05.060
of that particular
type of humor.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.720
And especially with a visual
medium like film for me

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:11.510
it was really a
journey to figure out

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.320
how to actually represent
that humor on screen,

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.550
so that it translates to
not only to audiences,

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:22.700
but through multiple
different cultures.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:23.300
That's great.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:24.570
Thank you.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.590
Ligiah, what about you?

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.450
I think for me the
thing that I always

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:33.920
have to keep going
back and forth about,

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:40.000
is how I see myself on the
inside, which is 100% Mexican,

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.120
and how I need to see
myself in the entertainment

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.190
industry, which is as a Latina.

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:53.150
And for me, a Latina, the word
Latino, or Latinos and now

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:56.180
Latinx, for example,
is not really a word

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:59.960
that we ever use in the
countries that we come from.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:01.280
We never say we're Latinos.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.710
We always say we're Mexican,
or we're Argentinian,

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:07.130
or we're Colombian,
or we're Brazilian.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:12.320
But in Hollywood, because
we have so little power,

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:18.080
and because our stories are seen
so rarely, a little bit more

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.970
in television now but
almost nonexistent in film,

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:25.200
unless the movie is coming
from another country.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.730
So I'm talking about the ability
for us who live in this country

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:30.380
to be able to tell our stories.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:33.690
That is almost nonexistent
in this country.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:39.730
The only way that we
can have some power

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:47.710
is by uniting with the word
Latina, or Latinx, or Latino.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.580
But for me I will
always be a Mexican.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.820
That's how I see myself,
that is my heart.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.760
And so it's navigating
that back and forth

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.130
and finding specificity.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:05.310
So that we don't just see a
Latinx character in television

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.920
and film, but then
we actually bring

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.600
in the specificity of what
that character may bring,

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.380
based on where they came from
or what their heritage is.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:18.320
All right.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:19.380
Thank you for that.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:22.010
Misan, what about you?

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:24.170
I think my--

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:28.820
I mean what I've always wanted
is to really expand what

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:31.040
it means to be Black on screen.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:36.590
That I found very
early on that we were--

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.900
as a Black writer I
was only really allowed

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:44.420
to inhabit quite small
spaces that there

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:50.840
was a lane that I was allowed to
be in, which was about you look

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:54.410
at the kinds of stories that
I would be allowed to tell,

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:56.750
and the kind of stories
that Black people would

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:59.890
be allowed to appear in.

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:03.560
And I was very
often told, when I

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:06.740
was developing Belle for
example, they would say,

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:10.490
oh, this just doesn't
feel like a Black story

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:15.500
because it's not for example
urban enough that it's not.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:26.180
Or there would be a real focus
on Black pain to the point

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.510
that it almost becomes that--

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:32.270
you say, what are you
actually trying to say?

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:38.090
So I think it's to expand the
stories we are allowed to tell

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:41.960
and stories that reflect
the entirety of who we

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:45.800
are as a people and as a race.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:49.940
And to expand as
well what people

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.580
would expect to
see when they look

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:55.910
at a film with a
Black person and not

00:09:55.910 --> 00:10:03.680
be defined by the stories that
people who for whatever reason,

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:07.010
those are the stories they feel
comfortable for us to be in.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.820
That we need to in the same
way as in the greater society,

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:13.520
we need to expand
the spaces where

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.700
people would expect to see
us and not be uncomfortable.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.110
We need to have that
on screen as well.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:27.350
And that has been my
quest in defining myself

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:28.670
as a storyteller.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.940
Is to be able to tell my
story and not the story

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:34.900
you expect me to tell.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:35.900
All right.

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:37.600
Thank you for that.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:38.820
And you, Máijá.

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.310
How would you define your voice?

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.400
Well, I think as an
Indigenous filmmaker,

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:48.120
as someone who's Blackfoot
and Sámi it's always about

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:52.110
centering myself in my
community and being responsible

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:55.200
and accountable to my own
community first and foremost

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.875
and thinking about my own
people as my audience.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:05.370
To speak about that idea of
expanding the types of stories

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:10.410
that can be told, I think it's
about thinking about the way

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:13.692
that Indigenous people have
been represented on screen.

00:11:13.692 --> 00:11:17.220
Since film first
started rolling it's

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.940
been decades and decades
of misrepresentation

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:23.500
and of outsiders
telling our stories.

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:28.080
So for me, it's about always
centering myself within my

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.690
within my communities
as an Indigenous person

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:34.850
and centering Indigenous
people as my audience.

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:36.702
And then I work from there.

00:11:36.702 --> 00:11:38.910
Well, I think you might be
being a little modest when

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:41.790
you're saying decades
of misrepresentation.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.220
more like a century perhaps.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:44.467
Yeah. let's use century.

00:11:44.467 --> 00:11:47.450
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:48.270
10 decades.

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:49.850
So let's say that.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.710
But I think all of us coming
from the distinct communities

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:55.470
that we're all coming
from, I think we

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:57.330
all share that in common.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:00.570
We all have that history
of misrepresentation

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.520
of our own peoples,
that I think definitely

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:06.720
affects I think, our
movement, our attempts to move

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:10.560
within a particular
industry and within a world

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:15.120
that I think has
preconceived notions of us.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:18.330
Misan, I heard you say it
in one of your lectures,

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:22.290
that you said the word
diversity is complicated.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:24.720
Because in order for
there to be diversity

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:30.545
you have to acknowledge what
the actual point of view is.

00:12:30.545 --> 00:12:32.670
How would you describe the
historical point of view

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:37.010
that I guess we're challenging.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:43.960
I think the point of view
that normality is white, male,

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:47.680
heterosexual, and
that their stories

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:50.490
are inherently interesting.

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:53.790
And there needs to
be no justification.

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:58.230
You just have to be and
your story is interesting.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:03.870
Whereas to convince
people that I then

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:05.380
have to be diverse from that.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:08.100
And once you say that,
you're immediately

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:11.760
saying that that is normal
and my stories are not.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.230
And some accommodation has to be
made in order to tell my story.

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.900
And you can end up
that telling my story

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:21.800
is like castor oil-- you
know, it's good for you,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:22.800
you've got to do it.

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:27.240
But actually, it's not pleasant.

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.350
It's not the norm.

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.640
And I think we need
to get away from that.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:37.590
The white male that I've
discussed at the beginning--

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:39.430
and nothing against it--

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:41.340
is diverse, to me.

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:45.600
So exactly-- they
are diverse to me.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.350
And the feeling
for those stories

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:52.320
are somehow
inherently interesting

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:56.490
and our stories have to
be justified, that is

00:13:56.490 --> 00:14:01.320
what I feel very strongly
against, because to me,

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.110
a lot of those stories are
specifically not interesting.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:06.240
It depends where
you stand before you

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:07.970
find something interesting.

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:12.480
And I'm worried about the
use of the word diversity,

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:15.990
because it entrenches a
particular point of view

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:19.410
that that is where
we are looking from,

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:23.280
and that all the rest
of us are diverse.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.100
I worry about that as a concept.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:30.090
Although, Misan, I have to say,
I was recently putting together

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.160
a writers' room, and
I said, you know,

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:34.710
maybe we should get a
white male in here in order

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:37.560
to have diversity,
because right now, we have

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.180
only women and women of color.

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:43.770
And everyone said, we don't
need that point of view.

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.260
Everyone knows that point
of view plenty fine.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:49.890
So we ended up not doing it.

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:51.557
I love that.

00:14:51.557 --> 00:14:53.640
Well, I do believe that
one of the things you know

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:56.970
we are commenting on is kind of
who determines the frame, who

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.790
determines what's centered,
who determines the norm.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.620
While we don't
acknowledge that enough,

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:07.170
I think also one of the
things that it definitely

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:11.070
leads to is creating bias.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:15.030
It creates historical
bias against all of us

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:18.270
you know who are not
represented in that norm

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:21.095
or maybe misrepresented
to that norm.

00:15:21.095 --> 00:15:22.470
But I just wondered
if anyone had

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:25.530
any ideas about what
are some steps we

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:27.810
can take to overcome that bias?

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.600
Because I think we do see it--

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:34.310
it's continually perpetuated.

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:36.380
Well, I would say
one is education.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:43.040
One thing that I always
say-- and I work much more

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:45.200
in television than
I work in features,

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:47.600
so I hope it's OK to talk
a little bit about that

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:49.670
experience in terms of feature--

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:51.900
but one of the
things that I say--

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.590
and I'm in the Equity
and Inclusion Committee

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:57.710
for the Writers
Guild of America,

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:00.470
and we have dealt with a
lot of issues of not just

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:06.170
sexual harassment, but
harassment and racism

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:07.470
in the writers' rooms.

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:11.270
And one thing that
I always say is

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:15.590
writers who get a
show for the most part

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:20.420
are being hired because
they are talented writers.

00:16:20.420 --> 00:16:24.000
They are incredible
storytellers.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.480
They are not getting
hired because they

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:29.710
have leadership skills.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:32.170
But they are put
in positions where

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:37.070
they have to be leaders of
not only the writers' room,

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:42.740
but an entire crew and
a whole group of actors.

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:48.980
And nobody has given them any
kind of information or classes

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:55.130
or leadership seminars of how
do you run a crew of 300 people?

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:58.400
What is acceptable
behavior and not?

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:01.700
What is considered
racist or not?

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:07.520
Who can help you navigate
these worlds and these stories

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:09.560
and these people?

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:12.410
And so now more
than ever, I think

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:15.200
it's super important, because
we no longer just have

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:18.230
four networks with
30 shows being

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:20.359
put on the air every year.

00:17:20.359 --> 00:17:24.680
We have dozens of cable
networks and streaming

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:28.910
services in addition
to network television.

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:33.680
We have 500 shows in
production a year.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:36.250
So we have a whole
generation of writers

00:17:36.250 --> 00:17:38.710
that are being put
in these positions

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:43.510
with absolutely no
knowledge of how to lead.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:47.290
And so a lot of the issues
that I think we see--

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:49.480
also in feature
sets, by the way,

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:52.060
because it's not like directors
are given leadership skills,

00:17:52.060 --> 00:17:53.590
either--

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:55.510
but more so I think
in television,

00:17:55.510 --> 00:17:57.850
because there's just
so many shows that

00:17:57.850 --> 00:17:59.680
are being produced a year.

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:04.300
That I think it's vital
to as part of getting

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:07.330
hired that you are
actually receiving

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:11.650
information and
training on these issues

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:16.810
as well as just some
leadership skills that

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:21.640
can guide you through a process
of running a room and beyond.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:22.970
I think that's important.

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:24.605
I agree with the
issue of training,

00:18:24.605 --> 00:18:25.730
because one of the things--

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:28.690
I think Elle-Máijá has a really
interesting story of when she

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:31.720
was you know co-directing
her first feature.

00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:36.190
She did a particular level
of training with her crew

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:39.640
and I think with actors and
with communities and everybody

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:42.850
who was really involved in the
creation and the production

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:43.780
of her feature film.

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:45.310
Can you describe
that a bit much?

00:18:45.310 --> 00:18:47.290
Because it was more
like around what

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:49.840
you would call, I guess you
said, cultural competency,

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:51.570
is that correct?

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:57.840
Yeah, so who is behind
the camera absolutely

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.420
shapes the narrative.

00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:03.780
And within the Indigenous film
community, we have quite a few

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:08.250
really talented writers and
directors and producers,

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:09.930
but we don't have
enough people working

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.510
in the technical departments.

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:14.850
We don't have enough people
working as sound recordists,

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:16.950
editors, all of those things.

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:22.680
And who makes up a crew
absolutely influences the story

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:24.430
that you're telling.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:26.730
And so as an
Indigenous filmmaker,

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:30.240
I thought it was so crucial
that we hire as many Indigenous

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.130
people within our
crew, especially

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:34.050
in those key
technical positions.

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:37.470
But what we're recognizing is
that there aren't enough of us

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:41.550
to be working on all the films
that are being made right now.

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:44.160
And so we took a
two-fold approach.

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:46.680
One was that we
recognized that we

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:49.020
had to hire a lot of
non-Indigenous crew members

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:51.160
who were lovely people.

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:52.890
But there's a lot of
work to do in terms

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:59.130
of unlearning
colonialism, unlearning

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:02.010
this complicated narrative
that we're all sitting in,

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.900
and finding new ways
of moving forward.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:08.550
So what we did was we held a
mandatory cultural competency

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:13.380
training, an Indigenous history
training for the entire crew

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:16.470
before we went into
production, which in a way,

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:19.770
was to just sort of ground
everybody in a place of respect

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:23.380
and understanding that we have
a lot of work to do together.

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:25.170
And the second thing
that we did was

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:29.160
that we hired 11 Indigenous
youth, young people wanting

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:32.460
to work in film in all of the
key technical departments,

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:34.980
in order to build capacity,
in order to move forward,

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:39.330
and to understand that
one key way of dismantling

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:42.510
these systems of
oppression and systems

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:48.420
of power that really shape the
kinds of stories that are being

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:52.500
told onscreen is to build
capacity within the Indigenous

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:54.630
film community itself.

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:55.200
Thank you.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:56.867
I think that's such
an interesting model

00:20:56.867 --> 00:20:59.940
to contemplate.

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.640
We have to acknowledge, I
think, in this moment in time,

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:06.000
given our pandemic and given
the state-sponsored terrorism

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:08.940
that's happening against
Black people in our country,

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:11.220
it's like we're
starting to recognize

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:13.860
these systems of power
and white supremacy that

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:16.530
exists not only in our political
systems in our country,

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:19.950
but I definitely think that
exist within our industry.

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:24.390
And I think if we're going
to continue to have an impact

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:26.610
and to create change, I
think those systems need

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:32.060
to be questioned more, and that
people need to be trained more.

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:35.050
Lulu, you were just
talking about having

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:36.660
staffed a writers' room.

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:40.560
Can you describe
how we all should

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.330
be getting into the room?

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:45.840
How did you get into the room
in order to get to this point

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.970
where you are?

00:21:47.970 --> 00:21:52.020
I mean, honestly, I've never
written in a room before,

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:58.170
so I was told by a couple
of my writers, they said,

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.730
this is unlike any room
I've ever experienced.

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:05.490
And I think in some ways,
because I didn't experience

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:10.820
the hierarchy and sort
of the abuse that is

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:15.180
so common in writers' rooms--

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:16.770
I said, we're a team.

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:18.750
Here's a group of
people who have

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:23.640
very different backgrounds,
very different experiences, who

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:25.710
are all tremendously
talented, so why

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:29.310
wouldn't we just talk
about everything?

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:33.510
I had to actually teach
some of the writers who

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.400
have been in a lot
of rooms and rooms

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:39.570
where they're not
allowed to speak up--

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:41.130
one woman kept raising her hand.

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:42.880
I was like, why are
you raising your hand?

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:44.580
Just talk.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:49.320
And so it is so strange to
kind of realize, here I am,

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:52.260
the showrunner, and I don't
have experience in a room.

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:56.730
So I would often turn to her
and say, having been in rooms,

00:22:56.730 --> 00:22:59.400
how is this normally done?

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:03.300
And then in other ways,
it was just organic.

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:06.420
You learn to be friends,
you ask each other

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:08.850
for their perspective.

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:11.280
And it always made
the story better--

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:12.960
always, right?

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:15.990
I think when people
are fearful and they

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:19.740
don't give their perspective,
that's not helping the show.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:22.560
That's going to hurt
me and the show.

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:25.050
If someone says, you
know, actually, I

00:23:25.050 --> 00:23:26.990
have the same background
as this character,

00:23:26.990 --> 00:23:28.980
this is not how we
would say things.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:31.890
I'm like please,
please, please tell me.

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:36.540
Do you want to take a look at
it, do you want to take a pass?

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:38.250
Call your father--
what would he say?

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:41.520
Like, all of those
things are what make

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:43.660
this story rich and authentic.

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:48.630
So I don't know why any
showrunner would ever

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:52.920
want to keep people
from expressing that.

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:55.620
That's just purely ego
and it's stupidity.

00:23:58.260 --> 00:23:59.300
I agree.

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:03.240
Misan, you came from a
background of being I believe,

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:04.170
a doctor.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:06.860
And were you a surgeon as well?

00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:09.570
Yeah, I was critical
care-- emergency room.

00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:13.650
Critical care before you
became a screenwriter.

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:15.810
I mean, what was that path like?

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:18.900
I mean, ultimately getting
to have your feature films

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:21.500
produced and made and shared?

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:23.040
There obviously
was a journey there

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:26.040
and you had to get into
those decision-making rooms.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:28.290
How did you do that?

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:30.430
Well, how I came
into screenwriting,

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:34.830
actually, dovetails very
well with this conversation

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:36.510
we're having today.

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:41.200
I went to the cinema
and I watched a film.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:44.220
And it was a film about slavery.

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:49.500
And there were no
Black people in it.

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:51.150
It was one of
these stories where

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:54.480
we knew slavery was
appalling and terrible,

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:59.830
because the white wife of
the white male character

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:04.510
kept having her had dinner
parties disrupted by his zeal

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:06.050
to end slavery.

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:08.080
And I just didn't
see any Black people.

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:12.250
And suddenly, I felt
that I never saw myself.

00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:17.242
If cinema is-- really, it's like
the home movie of the world,

00:25:17.242 --> 00:25:17.950
isn't it, really?

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:20.950
It's where we go and we see
ourselves, and we're not there.

00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:25.360
And when we are there, we're
horribly misrepresented.

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:26.260
But you don't know.

00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:31.540
You sit there sort of balancing
between the thrill of seeing

00:25:31.540 --> 00:25:35.620
yourself and the embarrassment
of how you're portrayed,

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:36.640
you know?

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:41.740
And I just got to the
point where I just

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:45.310
wanted to see something
that I would want to see.

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:48.593
I wanted to see myself
and I wrote a screenplay.

00:25:48.593 --> 00:25:50.510
It may seem sort of
rather an odd thing to do,

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:53.530
but as a doctor, obviously,
you tell stories all day.

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:57.310
So it isn't as far of a journey.

00:25:57.310 --> 00:26:00.940
But it was a desperate
yearning need

00:26:00.940 --> 00:26:05.920
to see myself represented
on screen in a way

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:07.450
that I recognized.

00:26:07.450 --> 00:26:11.695
And I think that many
of us feel that--

00:26:11.695 --> 00:26:14.900
that it's like being
a member of a family--

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:16.060
I said this before--

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:18.280
and you're watching the
home movies of the family

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:19.930
and you're not there.

00:26:19.930 --> 00:26:20.890
You're not there.

00:26:20.890 --> 00:26:23.600
You've been excluded
and you go, why?

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:26.690
And so that was how I started.

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:31.420
And that was the beginning
of my journey to write.

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:34.078
And I imagine you had to deal
with many gatekeepers, which

00:26:34.078 --> 00:26:35.620
is actually meant
to be that you know

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:38.260
the thrust of our
discussion today,

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:42.040
because I think we recognize
that there are decision makers.

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:43.480
There are gatekeepers.

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:48.040
There are the ones who make
the decisions to, I guess,

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:50.190
allow some of us in.

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:52.600
Ligiah, what has
been your experience

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:57.590
in navigating the gatekeeping,
I guess, of the industry?

00:26:57.590 --> 00:27:00.100
Well, I also didn't
start as a writer.

00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:02.410
I actually started
as an executive.

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:04.480
So I was an executive
in television

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:07.870
and I was also an
executive on features.

00:27:07.870 --> 00:27:10.840
I worked within a
studio system and I also

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:13.100
worked within a network system.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:17.130
And in every case, I
was the only Latina.

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:20.770
And sometimes, I was the only
Latina on the entire floor--

00:27:20.770 --> 00:27:24.220
not just even in my department,
but the entire floor.

00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:29.580
And so I always felt
I had a responsibility

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:34.170
in those positions to make
sure that the shows that I was

00:27:34.170 --> 00:27:39.450
overseeing or the movies
that I may be doing

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:42.870
had representation,
which wasn't the case.

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:46.050
So I understand
it from that side.

00:27:46.050 --> 00:27:48.270
And I and I understand
the importance

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:52.570
of having executives
of color and producers

00:27:52.570 --> 00:27:55.420
of color in those
positions that might

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:59.230
help us get through
the door, because I

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:02.020
have been that person.

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:07.010
At the same time, I think
that a lot of what could help

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:12.620
is for networks and for studios
to actually have a mandate.

00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:14.657
And I know they
hate that word and I

00:28:14.657 --> 00:28:16.490
know that they hate
using that, because they

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:19.670
feel like, no, we just
need to allow the filmmaker

00:28:19.670 --> 00:28:24.020
to be able to tell the stories
that they want to tell and work

00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:25.790
in their creative
environment or whatever,

00:28:25.790 --> 00:28:27.840
and I am all for that.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:32.750
But I think that a lot of
this has to come from the top.

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:37.340
They have to understand
that we are so far

00:28:37.340 --> 00:28:42.230
behind where we need to be
in terms of what the studios

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:44.300
and networks are
representing in television

00:28:44.300 --> 00:28:47.750
and what the world
actually looks like.

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:52.430
That unless they really start
mandating certain things,

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:57.590
I'm not sure things are
going to change drastically.

00:28:57.590 --> 00:29:01.550
But for me, I just feel
like I try to tell the best

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:02.930
stories that I can.

00:29:02.930 --> 00:29:06.380
One way that I have been
able to navigate this

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:12.500
is that I never set out to
only be a network writer

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:16.160
or I never set out to be
only a feature writer.

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:19.675
Like, I will only do
features or I will only

00:29:19.675 --> 00:29:21.050
work in network
television, which

00:29:21.050 --> 00:29:23.420
is not even a thing
anymore, because there's so

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:25.400
many streaming services.

00:29:25.400 --> 00:29:27.360
But at the time, it was.

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:31.730
So the way that I have
navigated this bias

00:29:31.730 --> 00:29:35.510
is for me not to think of
myself as just like I will only

00:29:35.510 --> 00:29:40.190
do primetime television or
I will only do features.

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:43.940
That was never really the way
that I thought about my career.

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:46.550
When I transitioned from
being a studio or network

00:29:46.550 --> 00:29:49.873
executive to being a
writer, one of the reasons

00:29:49.873 --> 00:29:51.290
why I did that is
because I wanted

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:54.080
to have control over the
stories that I was putting out

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:57.710
into the world, which I really
didn't as a network or studio

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:59.330
executive, right?

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:02.120
So when I made that
transition, I decided,

00:30:02.120 --> 00:30:05.270
if somebody wants to tell the
story in preschool animation,

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:07.610
I will do it in
preschool animation.

00:30:07.610 --> 00:30:10.400
If somebody wants to
do this as an MOW,

00:30:10.400 --> 00:30:13.760
meaning movie of the week, for
Hallmark Hall of Fame, which

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:17.120
is how I did the Alicia
Keys project, Firelight,

00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:21.180
I will do it in television
and for Hallmark Hall of Fame.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:26.000
So allowing myself
the opportunities that

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:29.210
go beyond where you
really usually think

00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:32.780
of where your story
should be told.

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:36.770
It's a way that I have been
able to navigate this bias.

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:40.490
That I have gone from
working on an NBC show

00:30:40.490 --> 00:30:43.190
to working on Go Diego, Go.

00:30:43.190 --> 00:30:45.500
And that was a preschool
show on Nickelodeon.

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:49.670
And everybody said, you
can not take that job.

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:52.760
You cannot do that job, because
you will never make it back

00:30:52.760 --> 00:30:54.770
to network television.

00:30:54.770 --> 00:30:56.980
And I thought to
myself, how do I

00:30:56.980 --> 00:31:00.950
say no to a show that is going
to have a Latino lead, that

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:05.660
is going to teach kids Spanish,
and the importance of science?

00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:09.890
Like, I literally could not
see a down side to that.

00:31:09.890 --> 00:31:14.270
And then I went from that to
releasing La Misma Luna, which

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:15.860
Fox Searchlight bought.

00:31:15.860 --> 00:31:18.380
So a lot of this
for me was like I

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:21.350
can't worry about what people
may think because I'm working

00:31:21.350 --> 00:31:24.620
in preschool TV or am I
doing a cable movie instead

00:31:24.620 --> 00:31:26.150
of a feature film.

00:31:26.150 --> 00:31:28.640
I just want to work
at the places that

00:31:28.640 --> 00:31:30.930
are willing to tell my stories.

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:33.842
And so instead of
always thinking about,

00:31:33.842 --> 00:31:36.050
oh, this story is never
going to be able to be told--

00:31:36.050 --> 00:31:38.180
no, I just go to
the places that are

00:31:38.180 --> 00:31:42.540
willing to tell the kinds of
stories that I want to do.

00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:46.160
And that has helped
me not go crazy

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:51.150
and not to think about the
negative aspects of what we do.

00:31:51.150 --> 00:31:54.470
But like I said
earlier, until the top

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:59.900
and the CEOs at these
companies really

00:31:59.900 --> 00:32:01.520
try to create some
sort of mandate

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:03.260
for what needs to
happen, I'm not

00:32:03.260 --> 00:32:05.780
sure how much is
going to change.

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:07.190
Thank you for that.

00:32:07.190 --> 00:32:11.830
I think you've been a master
at the navigation, obviously.

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:14.600
I wonder, does anybody
else have any comments,

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:18.015
I guess, about gatekeepering,
gatekeeping, and gatekeepers

00:32:18.015 --> 00:32:18.890
and how they've had--

00:32:18.890 --> 00:32:22.050
Bird, can I say one more thing?

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:26.010
Do you mind if I just
say one more thing?

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:28.880
I think that the
most powerful thing

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:32.700
that I have ever
done in my career

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:34.590
is to have the
courage to speak up.

00:32:37.450 --> 00:32:43.780
So a job was never worth
me not speaking up.

00:32:43.780 --> 00:32:45.920
And I think a lot
of us, because we

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:48.920
are the only ones in the room,
whether it's a writer's room

00:32:48.920 --> 00:32:51.620
or we're only in an
executive position

00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:55.760
or we're the only ones that
are getting the one Latino

00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:58.810
movie done in the next
three to five years,

00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:03.180
that sometimes, we're too
afraid to say something.

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:05.000
And I think that one thing--

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:08.120
and I'm old enough that maybe
that's giving me the courage--

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:10.700
but I was like this when I
was in network television,

00:33:10.700 --> 00:33:12.500
and I had to speak up.

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:14.150
We had a show taking
place in Miami

00:33:14.150 --> 00:33:17.030
and there were no Latinos,
not even in the background.

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:19.340
And I was like, you
can't do a show in Miami

00:33:19.340 --> 00:33:22.070
without any Latinos not
even on the background.

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:27.200
There were positions
that I took and continue

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:31.340
to take that I think are really
important for those of us who

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:33.200
are working and
for those of us who

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:36.590
are able to develop
and create programming,

00:33:36.590 --> 00:33:41.030
that our voices
need to be heard.

00:33:41.030 --> 00:33:43.190
One thing that I'm
very clear about when

00:33:43.190 --> 00:33:46.100
I go in, especially in
children's programming,

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:48.650
where a lot of times, they're
trying to make everybody

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:51.470
just be like everybody
else, I always

00:33:51.470 --> 00:33:56.990
say a character can not be
replaceable or interchangeable

00:33:56.990 --> 00:33:59.110
if it's a character of color.

00:33:59.110 --> 00:33:59.610
Yes.

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:01.910
So I don't want you
to just be able to put

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:04.050
anybody else in that role.

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:07.220
And I know that there is a
way, and some people do that,

00:34:07.220 --> 00:34:10.909
but being able to say
that, and then being clear,

00:34:10.909 --> 00:34:12.409
if you're going to
develop with me,

00:34:12.409 --> 00:34:15.570
this is the person that
you're actually going to get.

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:18.230
And you cannot assign me a
composer because you have been

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:21.770
working with that composer for
the last 10 shows that you did.

00:34:21.770 --> 00:34:25.639
You have to ask me if I have
an opinion about a composer

00:34:25.639 --> 00:34:28.880
of color that I might
want to use instead

00:34:28.880 --> 00:34:32.360
of the white composer that you
have been using for the last 20

00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:33.139
years.

00:34:33.139 --> 00:34:39.620
Like, being able to empower
yourself as a creator of color

00:34:39.620 --> 00:34:41.705
and not allow these
things to continue

00:34:41.705 --> 00:34:43.580
because you are afraid
that your shows aren't

00:34:43.580 --> 00:34:45.170
going to make it on the air.

00:34:45.170 --> 00:34:48.960
Unless we speak up, these
things are going to continue.

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:51.500
So the way that I see
it is even if I am--

00:34:51.500 --> 00:34:53.389
let's suppose that
I'm fired, which

00:34:53.389 --> 00:34:55.170
hasn't happened that
often, by the way--

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:57.139
but let's suppose
that I am fired--

00:34:57.139 --> 00:35:00.110
I can promise you that that
executive or that producer

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:04.020
is not going to forget
that conversation.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:08.310
And that might bring change
to somebody else in another

00:35:08.310 --> 00:35:10.380
project that is not
even related to me,

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:13.365
because they're always going to
remember the outspoken Ligiah,

00:35:13.365 --> 00:35:19.260
Mexicana, that came in here and
told us, you cannot do this.

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:26.250
And so I feel strongly that
those of us who are working

00:35:26.250 --> 00:35:31.100
must have the courage to
speak up in these situations.

00:35:31.100 --> 00:35:33.770
Thank you.

00:35:33.770 --> 00:35:41.540
If I might say, I think also, at
this moment, one sort of feels

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:44.870
exhausted by this
process and it's time

00:35:44.870 --> 00:35:49.040
to stop asking nicely as though
we were asking for a favor,

00:35:49.040 --> 00:35:52.100
and try and change perception
that what we are doing

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:56.120
is just demanding
normal rights, you know?

00:35:56.120 --> 00:36:00.110
And not feel all
the time that one

00:36:00.110 --> 00:36:06.200
has to somehow persuade people,
but rather, look at the wider

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:12.470
society that will accept that.

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:17.870
Change things so that people
accept that the present status

00:36:17.870 --> 00:36:21.500
quo is inherently wrong.

00:36:21.500 --> 00:36:24.950
And therefore, it's how are
we going to put it right--

00:36:24.950 --> 00:36:30.080
not how are we going to
have the favors dispensed.

00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:32.190
Our stories matter.

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:35.810
You know, Black stories, Latina
stories, Indigenous stories--

00:36:35.810 --> 00:36:37.270
our stories matter.

00:36:37.270 --> 00:36:38.960
Our representation matter.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:40.520
Our lives matter.

00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:46.550
And we need that to be inherent,
not just in our industry,

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:48.500
but in the wider society.

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:52.010
Because people need
to understand that

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:56.030
the present status
quo is unsustainable.

00:36:56.030 --> 00:36:58.405
And it is very difficult,
and I agree with you,

00:36:58.405 --> 00:37:03.410
because sometimes, it's
very difficult to speak out

00:37:03.410 --> 00:37:05.610
when you're a writer.

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:07.310
And from my point
of view, when you're

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:11.900
getting notes, which some
notes can be just horrendous.

00:37:11.900 --> 00:37:15.200
And you think, if
I say anything,

00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:18.240
is it better if I'm working
at it from the inside and all

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:18.740
of that?

00:37:18.740 --> 00:37:23.090
And after some point, we're
just exhausted with it.

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:28.450
And we need to be heard and
our stories need to be heard.

00:37:28.450 --> 00:37:34.520
And we can't do it alone without
the wider society accepting

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:38.570
and understanding that
the current status quo is

00:37:38.570 --> 00:37:41.120
unsustainable and unacceptable.

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:41.975
Yes.

00:37:41.975 --> 00:37:43.850
Lulu, can you share
us a point of view

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:47.540
on navigating with this
world, with gatekeepers

00:37:47.540 --> 00:37:50.630
and your talent?

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:54.740
Yeah, I think one
of the things I

00:37:54.740 --> 00:38:00.880
tell so many filmmaker friends
of mine who are women of color

00:38:00.880 --> 00:38:03.140
is--

00:38:03.140 --> 00:38:05.420
we talk a lot about do
you build your own table,

00:38:05.420 --> 00:38:08.280
do you try to get a
seat at the table.

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:12.170
We have these debates of we just
gotta say forget the system,

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:14.090
and just build our own table.

00:38:14.090 --> 00:38:16.650
But you have to
have the numbers.

00:38:16.650 --> 00:38:19.430
You have to have power in
order to go and build a table.

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:23.030
And the generations
that came before us,

00:38:23.030 --> 00:38:24.480
they didn't have that choice.

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:26.990
They had to find a
seat at the table

00:38:26.990 --> 00:38:31.670
and asked to be let in
order to build their clout,

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:35.090
their, whatever,
reputation, all of it,

00:38:35.090 --> 00:38:40.220
in order to go and maybe
create their own table.

00:38:40.220 --> 00:38:45.620
And so I think
that for me, I have

00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:49.580
to take this perspective of
abundance, which is something

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:52.470
that we all have to
unlearn, of not thinking

00:38:52.470 --> 00:38:55.460
of ourselves as
special or singular,

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:58.010
and there's only room for one.

00:38:58.010 --> 00:39:03.950
Because as everyone comes up,
that's how we have more power,

00:39:03.950 --> 00:39:04.760
create--

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:09.110
those are the people that
you can turn to and say--

00:39:09.110 --> 00:39:12.180
because when you're there
alone, it's terrible.

00:39:12.180 --> 00:39:14.330
It's like you don't
know if you're crazy

00:39:14.330 --> 00:39:16.280
or they're crazy,
because they're

00:39:16.280 --> 00:39:18.330
convincing you that you're
the one who's crazy.

00:39:18.330 --> 00:39:21.140
But if you have other
people around who

00:39:21.140 --> 00:39:24.300
had this other perspective,
it's just better.

00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:27.380
And so one of the things I
talk about with these friends

00:39:27.380 --> 00:39:31.430
is transparency, where
anybody who asks me,

00:39:31.430 --> 00:39:33.980
I'll show them my contracts.

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:36.980
I ask friends of mine to
show me their contracts.

00:39:36.980 --> 00:39:40.160
I think it's really
important that we

00:39:40.160 --> 00:39:43.807
have comps with
other filmmakers who

00:39:43.807 --> 00:39:45.640
have done a first
feature, a second feature,

00:39:45.640 --> 00:39:48.500
are doing their first TV show,
going, well, what did you make?

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:50.630
And what rights were you given?

00:39:50.630 --> 00:39:52.710
Where you allowed
to pick your DP,

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:57.260
were you allowed to pick
the writers in your room.

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:00.980
So I always tell my friends
not to get overly excited

00:40:00.980 --> 00:40:02.390
when they get offered a deal.

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:05.020
Before you get excited,
do your research.

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:06.830
Talk to as many
people as possible

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:08.660
who have worked
with this company.

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:11.660
Find out how they
treat people of color,

00:40:11.660 --> 00:40:16.580
how they treat both
financially and creatively.

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:22.670
And that way, they are
sort of armed with facts

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:25.760
to go back to their
reps to say, I'm

00:40:25.760 --> 00:40:27.060
not going to take this deal.

00:40:27.060 --> 00:40:29.000
I'm not going to
come to the table

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:33.250
and be grateful, which is
what they're expecting.

00:40:33.250 --> 00:40:35.640
And so I said, don't
stop getting excited,

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:38.505
stop being grateful,
and make sure

00:40:38.505 --> 00:40:39.630
that they're actually val--

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:44.260
valuing?

00:40:44.260 --> 00:40:46.590
Sorry, I just had a brain fart.

00:40:46.590 --> 00:40:51.630
They're actually giving as
much value as you deserve.

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:55.320
And unfortunately, sometimes,
that means you have to say no.

00:40:55.320 --> 00:40:57.240
And that's something
I talk a lot about,

00:40:57.240 --> 00:40:59.970
as well-- is the
power of saying no.

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:02.370
Because before I
made The Farewell,

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:04.350
I was just saying
yes to everyone

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:08.730
because I was so desperate to
make a story, to make a film,

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:11.380
and get it out somehow.

00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:17.640
But there is such an
emotional deficit.

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:21.270
Like, there's a payment that
goes, and nothing is for free.

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:23.580
And so you might
say yes to somebody,

00:41:23.580 --> 00:41:26.730
and they turn your story
into something else.

00:41:26.730 --> 00:41:30.540
You're not properly
paid to do this work.

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:32.850
You can burn out so quickly.

00:41:32.850 --> 00:41:38.160
And so I think that the
importance of having

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:43.050
the confidence and the value
in your own work to say,

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:47.640
I'm not going to do this work
unless the proper conditions

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:52.440
are met, because I can't do my
best work if these conditions

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:53.400
are not met.

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:57.360
And if I can't do my best
work, when the work comes out,

00:41:57.360 --> 00:42:00.180
and people go, well look,
here's an Asian woman,

00:42:00.180 --> 00:42:04.410
and we gave it a shot, we
did this film, but it failed,

00:42:04.410 --> 00:42:06.840
it's going to be put on you.

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:09.240
And so that's the
first thing I say

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:14.640
to anybody who works with me,
is like, if you value what I do,

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:17.280
these are the terms
in which I need

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:21.240
to do my best work, including
bringing my own collaborators,

00:42:21.240 --> 00:42:24.480
who may not have the same
credits, who you may not

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:26.740
be as familiar with.

00:42:26.740 --> 00:42:30.340
But this is the
organic process for me.

00:42:30.340 --> 00:42:32.730
And if I can't have my
process, then you're

00:42:32.730 --> 00:42:36.570
not going to get the result
that you're looking for.

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:37.530
Thank you.

00:42:37.530 --> 00:42:40.890
Elle-Máijá, I wanted to give you
a chance to comment before we

00:42:40.890 --> 00:42:43.210
may have to move
on to questions.

00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:43.710
Sure.

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:48.810
Yeah, I'm loving
listening to everybody.

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:50.700
I really appreciate
what Lulu said

00:42:50.700 --> 00:42:53.780
about this notion of abundance.

00:42:53.780 --> 00:42:58.590
About thinking differently about
our stories, about stories that

00:42:58.590 --> 00:43:00.750
come from BIPOC communities,
about stories that

00:43:00.750 --> 00:43:05.520
come from voices that have
been marginalized or silenced

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:08.760
or erased within the film world.

00:43:08.760 --> 00:43:11.250
And I really appreciate
what she said

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:16.020
about building a new process
and about considering

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:19.890
who these gatekeepers are.

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:21.510
So when it came to
The Body Remembers

00:43:21.510 --> 00:43:25.230
When The World Broke Open, it's
a film about two Indigenous

00:43:25.230 --> 00:43:27.720
women in East
Vancouver in Canada,

00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:29.890
it covers issues of
domestic violence,

00:43:29.890 --> 00:43:35.610
and it's a bit of a heavy film,
and we had a really hard time

00:43:35.610 --> 00:43:36.990
closing our financing gap.

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:40.890
And we had a really hard
time finding a distributor,

00:43:40.890 --> 00:43:44.960
because people continually
said, this story is important,

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:46.580
we understand it's
an important story,

00:43:46.580 --> 00:43:48.538
but we don't know how
we're going to market it.

00:43:48.538 --> 00:43:51.260
We don't know how we're
going to sell this story.

00:43:51.260 --> 00:43:53.660
And we kept getting that
same sort of response.

00:43:53.660 --> 00:43:57.860
And then the film got in front
of Ava DuVernay and Tilane

00:43:57.860 --> 00:44:00.950
Jones at ARRAY Releasing.

00:44:00.950 --> 00:44:03.500
And they decided to pick up
the film and that changed

00:44:03.500 --> 00:44:04.460
everything for us.

00:44:04.460 --> 00:44:07.130
And I think what
that speaks to is

00:44:07.130 --> 00:44:09.050
the fact that when
there are people

00:44:09.050 --> 00:44:13.820
who come from marginalized
communities, who are BIPOC,

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:16.970
in these positions of power,
people who are able to shift

00:44:16.970 --> 00:44:21.590
the narrative, and to show that
there is an audience for films

00:44:21.590 --> 00:44:25.460
like mine and for the
others on the panel,

00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:27.120
it changes everything.

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.750
And so now that film
is available on Netflix

00:44:29.750 --> 00:44:31.730
to such a broad audience.

00:44:31.730 --> 00:44:34.610
And what we're finding is that
despite the fact that it's

00:44:34.610 --> 00:44:38.000
such a specific story
about two Indigenous women,

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:39.800
it has so many universal themes.

00:44:39.800 --> 00:44:42.050
And there's so many people
who are drawn to the story

00:44:42.050 --> 00:44:44.510
on a personal level
who aren't Indigenous,

00:44:44.510 --> 00:44:46.850
who aren't from Canada.

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:49.760
And so it really
is about shifting

00:44:49.760 --> 00:44:51.650
who is in these
positions of power.

00:44:51.650 --> 00:44:58.670
It's about holding gatekeepers
accountable, and acknowledging

00:44:58.670 --> 00:45:01.940
that as long as
there are straight,

00:45:01.940 --> 00:45:05.870
cisgendered, white men in
positions of power deciding

00:45:05.870 --> 00:45:08.990
whose stories get told
then who's don't, we're

00:45:08.990 --> 00:45:13.040
going to continue to see
the same issues replicated

00:45:13.040 --> 00:45:15.050
and the same voices
being silenced.

00:45:15.050 --> 00:45:19.880
So I have so much gratitude
for people like Ava and Tilane

00:45:19.880 --> 00:45:25.340
at ARRAY, because they're
just opening so many doors

00:45:25.340 --> 00:45:28.700
for voices like mine.

00:45:28.700 --> 00:45:33.260
And I think that really
speaks to a brighter

00:45:33.260 --> 00:45:34.530
future for all of us.

00:45:34.530 --> 00:45:38.180
And I hope that this moment that
we're experiencing right now

00:45:38.180 --> 00:45:43.040
in terms of a reckoning with
race relations in the States

00:45:43.040 --> 00:45:44.030
and also in Canada--

00:45:44.030 --> 00:45:47.210
I hope that the work
that's being done right now

00:45:47.210 --> 00:45:48.710
continues into the future.

00:45:48.710 --> 00:45:52.250
And I really hope we see
lasting change and lasting

00:45:52.250 --> 00:45:53.905
accountability.

00:45:53.905 --> 00:45:55.280
You know what--
I also think it's

00:45:55.280 --> 00:46:02.110
important for artists who
are not a person of color,

00:46:02.110 --> 00:46:05.950
for them to also be
thoughtful about the projects

00:46:05.950 --> 00:46:09.180
that they take on.

00:46:09.180 --> 00:46:11.380
Are they asking a question
when they are brought in

00:46:11.380 --> 00:46:17.030
to do a Latino story, have
you met with Latino writers?

00:46:17.030 --> 00:46:21.310
Have you met with
Latino directors?

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:25.420
That they themselves
also look at this journey

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:28.155
as their journey, as well.

00:46:28.155 --> 00:46:29.530
And I think that
everybody should

00:46:29.530 --> 00:46:31.820
be able to tell the stories
that they want to tell.

00:46:31.820 --> 00:46:35.980
So I have a different
opinion about a person

00:46:35.980 --> 00:46:39.130
who may be passionate about a
story that they want to tell.

00:46:39.130 --> 00:46:41.740
I'm talking about dealing
with these gatekeepers

00:46:41.740 --> 00:46:43.090
at the studios.

00:46:43.090 --> 00:46:48.600
If you are being brought in to
do a story that is not really

00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:52.920
your own, are you asking
questions yourself?

00:46:52.920 --> 00:46:56.640
So not just ask asking
those questions.

00:46:56.640 --> 00:47:00.180
It's so sad for me to know
that there are two big Latino

00:47:00.180 --> 00:47:03.690
movies that are going
to be released this year

00:47:03.690 --> 00:47:07.430
and neither one is being
directed by a Latino.

00:47:07.430 --> 00:47:09.350
And thank god that one
of them is directed

00:47:09.350 --> 00:47:13.520
by a person of color, but we
are still having our stories

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:17.030
told by non-Latinos.

00:47:17.030 --> 00:47:20.720
And so we have a
responsibility to speak out,

00:47:20.720 --> 00:47:25.400
but I think hopefully the
community as a whole is also

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:28.400
speaking up about these
issues and saying,

00:47:28.400 --> 00:47:31.880
who have you met on this?

00:47:31.880 --> 00:47:36.830
It's so interesting-- I think
four of the last six Academy

00:47:36.830 --> 00:47:42.340
Awards for director have
been given to a Mexican,

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:45.490
but we still can't
find directors

00:47:45.490 --> 00:47:48.790
that can direct the movies
that the studios want

00:47:48.790 --> 00:47:51.750
to make about us.

00:47:51.750 --> 00:47:56.130
It's just the irony of
it is incredible to me.

00:47:56.130 --> 00:47:57.330
I love that you're saying--

00:47:57.330 --> 00:48:01.830
it's interesting, because I'm
just thinking as a metaphor,

00:48:01.830 --> 00:48:05.760
as an analogy, when we travel,
at least when I travel,

00:48:05.760 --> 00:48:10.800
or when I go out to LA to go
to a restaurant, I'm like,

00:48:10.800 --> 00:48:12.540
let's go to that
Vietnamese restaurant.

00:48:12.540 --> 00:48:14.190
It's full of Vietnamese people.

00:48:14.190 --> 00:48:17.460
That's a good sign when it's
filled with Vietnamese people

00:48:17.460 --> 00:48:18.150
or wherever.

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:21.480
If you're traveling, you want
to go to the local places

00:48:21.480 --> 00:48:24.480
and you look to make sure that
not only the people cooking,

00:48:24.480 --> 00:48:27.840
but the people eating are
actually from that background.

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:29.550
And it gives it legitimacy.

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:33.930
And how do we not do that with
movies, you know what I mean?

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:36.360
If I want to see
a Mexican film, I

00:48:36.360 --> 00:48:38.550
want to make sure that
the people cooking

00:48:38.550 --> 00:48:41.460
the meal and the people
who are coming to the meal

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:44.250
are giving it the
validation, because that's

00:48:44.250 --> 00:48:51.090
saying to me that that's
an authentic experience.

00:48:51.090 --> 00:48:54.480
And I talk a lot with
friends about my experience

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:58.560
when I get notes from people who
don't come from my background.

00:48:58.560 --> 00:49:01.240
how do I say it?

00:49:01.240 --> 00:49:04.090
How do I say, you
don't get this--

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:07.630
you don't get it, because
you're not from where I'm from.

00:49:07.630 --> 00:49:11.170
And it took me a long time
to actually find those words,

00:49:11.170 --> 00:49:12.670
because you still
have to be polite.

00:49:12.670 --> 00:49:14.952
You can't just come straight
out and be like, hey,

00:49:14.952 --> 00:49:16.660
if you don't let me
do it, that's racist.

00:49:16.660 --> 00:49:19.030
Like, you can't really say
that, right, to an exec.

00:49:19.030 --> 00:49:22.870
So there's this
diplomatic way of saying,

00:49:22.870 --> 00:49:25.450
I really respect
your opinion, but I

00:49:25.450 --> 00:49:27.460
don't think this is a story no.

00:49:27.460 --> 00:49:29.830
I think this is a
cultural no that you

00:49:29.830 --> 00:49:31.540
don't understand
because you don't

00:49:31.540 --> 00:49:33.340
come from where I come from.

00:49:33.340 --> 00:49:36.640
And they really respond
well-- at least producers

00:49:36.640 --> 00:49:38.830
that I've worked with
really respond well to that.

00:49:38.830 --> 00:49:40.640
But I think you're
right, Ligiah.

00:49:40.640 --> 00:49:43.130
I wish that
executives, producers,

00:49:43.130 --> 00:49:46.630
people who are working
with creators that

00:49:46.630 --> 00:49:48.610
are from different
backgrounds don't

00:49:48.610 --> 00:49:52.530
wait until they say to you--

00:49:52.530 --> 00:49:56.940
because it's a lot to
carry all of that pressure.

00:49:56.940 --> 00:49:59.220
For them to come to
you and say, hey, you

00:49:59.220 --> 00:50:00.430
don't come from where I come.

00:50:00.430 --> 00:50:04.620
Like, you should recognize that
and say that right up front

00:50:04.620 --> 00:50:11.280
and give them the space so
that the creator can point when

00:50:11.280 --> 00:50:16.050
something is a cultural
difference and a perspective

00:50:16.050 --> 00:50:21.530
difference rather than a
purely technical craft no, you

00:50:21.530 --> 00:50:22.850
know what I'm saying?

00:50:22.850 --> 00:50:25.140
Yeah, and also,
looking at your film,

00:50:25.140 --> 00:50:28.890
I was thinking about when
I watched it, sometimes,

00:50:28.890 --> 00:50:31.410
even though it is a cultural
thing that people may not

00:50:31.410 --> 00:50:35.040
understand, sometimes,
it is in the specificity

00:50:35.040 --> 00:50:37.470
that you find commonality.

00:50:37.470 --> 00:50:40.820
So I remember when I
went to see your film,

00:50:40.820 --> 00:50:46.160
that a whole cemetery scene
with the crying and the food,

00:50:46.160 --> 00:50:48.770
and it was like Día
de los Muertos for us.

00:50:48.770 --> 00:50:51.110
Like, it is crazy.

00:50:51.110 --> 00:50:53.220
And I don't know if
you guys know this,

00:50:53.220 --> 00:50:59.720
but Coco is the highest
grossing Pixar movie in China.

00:50:59.720 --> 00:51:03.240
I think it made $200
million in China.

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:06.830
And I was like, I
wonder why Coco made

00:51:06.830 --> 00:51:09.210
so much money in China, right?

00:51:09.210 --> 00:51:13.160
And it's like Coco can
not be more specific.

00:51:13.160 --> 00:51:16.940
It is so specifically Mexican.

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:20.990
But there's a whole group of
people that saw this movie

00:51:20.990 --> 00:51:25.310
and found commonality in
the specificity of it.

00:51:25.310 --> 00:51:28.930
And sometimes, what executives
are really afraid of

00:51:28.930 --> 00:51:31.850
is the thing that they
should fear the least,

00:51:31.850 --> 00:51:34.820
because people will find
their way through the movies.

00:51:34.820 --> 00:51:36.740
They will have
some understanding.

00:51:36.740 --> 00:51:39.720
The same thing happened with
Under the Same Moon in Jordan,

00:51:39.720 --> 00:51:42.110
where I was thinking,
how are people going

00:51:42.110 --> 00:51:44.090
to relate to this in Jordan?

00:51:44.090 --> 00:51:47.000
Well, they were
experiencing a huge amount

00:51:47.000 --> 00:51:50.600
of people coming in from Syria.

00:51:50.600 --> 00:51:54.680
And all of a sudden, there was
so much commonality in that.

00:51:54.680 --> 00:51:57.320
And so I would hope
that also, it's

00:51:57.320 --> 00:51:59.270
something that they look at.

00:51:59.270 --> 00:52:03.240
I saw your movie, Lulu,
and I immediately connected

00:52:03.240 --> 00:52:06.470
with so many of the things,
even though we could not

00:52:06.470 --> 00:52:09.590
be more far apart in
terms of our cultures

00:52:09.590 --> 00:52:11.360
or our backgrounds.

00:52:11.360 --> 00:52:13.290
Thank you.

00:52:13.290 --> 00:52:14.660
I loved your movie, as well.

00:52:18.800 --> 00:52:21.170
I think one of the
things that exercises

00:52:21.170 --> 00:52:25.130
me is, how do we stop
this being our problem,

00:52:25.130 --> 00:52:28.700
we have to overcome.

00:52:28.700 --> 00:52:33.810
Because actually, the reality
is, it is not our problem.

00:52:33.810 --> 00:52:36.140
It's the problem with
the people around us.

00:52:36.140 --> 00:52:41.810
And how do we make sure that
even the perspective from which

00:52:41.810 --> 00:52:45.590
we look at what to be
done, what has to be done

00:52:45.590 --> 00:52:51.110
isn't just focused on us having
to be super women or do this

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:56.150
and how do we overcome, but how
do we change the conversation

00:52:56.150 --> 00:52:58.760
to, what are you doing?

00:52:58.760 --> 00:53:06.530
I mean, you are
wrong, therefore,

00:53:06.530 --> 00:53:10.370
how are we going to
involve you in the solution

00:53:10.370 --> 00:53:14.060
and not have it about us
having to overcome you?

00:53:14.060 --> 00:53:16.150
How do you make you
part of the solution?

00:53:16.150 --> 00:53:20.900
And I think when we talk
about gatekeepers, obviously,

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:25.980
having more diverse--

00:53:25.980 --> 00:53:26.960
I've used the word--

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:29.450
gatekeepers is going
to make a difference.

00:53:29.450 --> 00:53:34.260
But there is also the
majority population

00:53:34.260 --> 00:53:37.930
that they have to
be part of this.

00:53:37.930 --> 00:53:44.570
And we can do things
like mandating things,

00:53:44.570 --> 00:53:46.500
as we discussed earlier.

00:53:46.500 --> 00:53:49.790
But there's also somehow,
that hearts and minds

00:53:49.790 --> 00:53:51.530
have to be changed, right?

00:53:51.530 --> 00:53:55.520
That's what sort of exercises
me, because people also

00:53:55.520 --> 00:53:56.450
have to be willing.

00:53:56.450 --> 00:54:00.230
Otherwise, what happens
is that they'll make that.

00:54:00.230 --> 00:54:02.580
I remember being told,
a filmmaker here,

00:54:02.580 --> 00:54:06.040
oh, we already
made a Black film.

00:54:06.040 --> 00:54:10.070
They made one three years
before, and so that's that.

00:54:10.070 --> 00:54:13.160
As opposed to thinking,
this is just normal.

00:54:13.160 --> 00:54:15.770
This is normal life
and this is just how

00:54:15.770 --> 00:54:19.010
the world when I step foot
outside my door looks like.

00:54:19.010 --> 00:54:21.770
And this is what it
should look like here.

00:54:21.770 --> 00:54:24.770
And I can understand that your
stories are interesting to you,

00:54:24.770 --> 00:54:26.270
my stories are
interesting to me.

00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:27.560
Let's make them all.

00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:30.260
That's what exercises me--

00:54:30.260 --> 00:54:34.520
is how do we stop approaching
this is our problem,

00:54:34.520 --> 00:54:42.380
and also focus on
how we can make

00:54:42.380 --> 00:54:45.230
it a wider society
or wider problem

00:54:45.230 --> 00:54:47.030
within the industry,
and not something

00:54:47.030 --> 00:54:49.670
that we have to overcome.

00:54:49.670 --> 00:54:51.590
But that there should
not be that resistance

00:54:51.590 --> 00:54:53.600
in the first place.

00:54:53.600 --> 00:54:56.530
Maybe in Utopia, but
that's what I think

00:54:56.530 --> 00:55:00.010
we should be driving towards.

00:55:00.010 --> 00:55:02.500
It's exhausting-- can I
just say, when you go in

00:55:02.500 --> 00:55:07.728
and pitch a story, and
the first half hour,

00:55:07.728 --> 00:55:10.270
you're not pitching your story,
you're not pitching yourself.

00:55:10.270 --> 00:55:12.310
You're pitching your relevance.

00:55:12.310 --> 00:55:18.640
And I had a most wonderful
sort of experience

00:55:18.640 --> 00:55:23.260
of pitching to an
African-American woman

00:55:23.260 --> 00:55:26.200
executive, and it was
like suddenly-- it

00:55:26.200 --> 00:55:30.280
was like having the weights
on your feet, the anvil that's

00:55:30.280 --> 00:55:34.670
being held on your feet gone,
because I could suddenly just

00:55:34.670 --> 00:55:35.170
pitch.

00:55:35.170 --> 00:55:36.970
Pitch my story,
pitch what it was

00:55:36.970 --> 00:55:39.610
without this sort of preamble.

00:55:39.610 --> 00:55:42.010
And it's to how do we get
there where there isn't

00:55:42.010 --> 00:55:44.050
that resistance in
the first place,

00:55:44.050 --> 00:55:48.400
rather than us having to
always be so special in order

00:55:48.400 --> 00:55:51.870
to be able to get through.

00:55:51.870 --> 00:55:54.520
In Canada, we're kind of
in this unique position,

00:55:54.520 --> 00:55:58.030
and we've modeled what's
happened in Australia and New

00:55:58.030 --> 00:56:00.940
Zealand in terms of
Indigenous stories, Indigenous

00:56:00.940 --> 00:56:02.410
representation.

00:56:02.410 --> 00:56:05.060
Indigenous people are
leading the conversation

00:56:05.060 --> 00:56:07.090
and we're seeing
that now in Canada.

00:56:07.090 --> 00:56:10.330
We have the Indigenous Screen
Office, and a lot of work

00:56:10.330 --> 00:56:13.870
has been placed into developing
something called the Pathways

00:56:13.870 --> 00:56:16.030
and Protocols Document.

00:56:16.030 --> 00:56:20.767
And it's literally a guide
for non-Indigenous people

00:56:20.767 --> 00:56:22.600
wanting to work with
Indigenous communities,

00:56:22.600 --> 00:56:25.660
wanting to tell stories
with Indigenous content.

00:56:25.660 --> 00:56:29.620
And in a lot of ways,
it's a way of holding

00:56:29.620 --> 00:56:31.210
non-Indigenous
people accountable,

00:56:31.210 --> 00:56:35.110
because it's trendy right now
to tell Indigenous stories.

00:56:35.110 --> 00:56:40.930
Like, every week, I get
multiple emails, multiple calls

00:56:40.930 --> 00:56:44.110
requesting that I
direct or help write

00:56:44.110 --> 00:56:46.950
the story that has an Indigenous
character or Indigenous

00:56:46.950 --> 00:56:48.460
whatever.

00:56:48.460 --> 00:56:51.010
And it's such an
incredible resource

00:56:51.010 --> 00:56:54.220
to have this Pathways and
Protocols Document to say,

00:56:54.220 --> 00:56:56.500
here, look at this
document, read it.

00:56:56.500 --> 00:56:58.120
This is what you need to do.

00:56:58.120 --> 00:57:00.830
And these are the questions
you need to ask yourself.

00:57:00.830 --> 00:57:02.350
Should you be
telling this story?

00:57:02.350 --> 00:57:04.660
Are you the right person
to tell this story?

00:57:04.660 --> 00:57:07.060
Have you connected with
community communities

00:57:07.060 --> 00:57:11.290
in a responsible,
ethical, meaningful way?

00:57:11.290 --> 00:57:16.130
And if they haven't, they
have that document to look to.

00:57:16.130 --> 00:57:19.060
And so I think we
need to continue

00:57:19.060 --> 00:57:21.700
to move in that
direction when it comes

00:57:21.700 --> 00:57:25.150
to telling all stories
that are relevant

00:57:25.150 --> 00:57:30.990
or represent BIPOC communities
or LGBTQ2S communities,

00:57:30.990 --> 00:57:32.690
or the disabled community.

00:57:32.690 --> 00:57:36.610
I think there needs to be
some sort of this mandated

00:57:36.610 --> 00:57:41.720
documents in a way that
serve us as a tool,

00:57:41.720 --> 00:57:44.980
because there's so much
emotional labor, so much

00:57:44.980 --> 00:57:49.300
education that we have
to do as the other

00:57:49.300 --> 00:57:51.730
when it comes to
helping our own stories.

00:57:51.730 --> 00:57:54.490
And having that document,
having that tool

00:57:54.490 --> 00:57:58.420
has been such a such a
weight off my shoulders

00:57:58.420 --> 00:58:00.080
as an Indigenous storyteller.

00:58:00.080 --> 00:58:04.150
And I think it also protects
our communities, because there's

00:58:04.150 --> 00:58:08.620
kind of this toxic form
of extractive filmmaking

00:58:08.620 --> 00:58:11.740
that has existed since the
cameras first started rolling.

00:58:11.740 --> 00:58:15.520
So people of color,
Indigenous people,

00:58:15.520 --> 00:58:19.960
anybody who is the other has
been fetishized or romanticized

00:58:19.960 --> 00:58:23.560
or misrepresented in
front of the camera.

00:58:23.560 --> 00:58:29.830
And there's just so much work
to do to unlearn all of that,

00:58:29.830 --> 00:58:32.820
to change all of those
ways of operating.

00:58:32.820 --> 00:58:35.740
And when it comes to, like
I said, this extractive form

00:58:35.740 --> 00:58:38.950
of filmmaking, it
comes from people

00:58:38.950 --> 00:58:41.200
who are not from those
communities going in

00:58:41.200 --> 00:58:44.350
with a voyeuristic lens,
and getting it all wrong

00:58:44.350 --> 00:58:45.310
every single time.

00:58:45.310 --> 00:58:47.170
They always get it wrong.

00:58:47.170 --> 00:58:49.750
And so it needs to
shift to us being

00:58:49.750 --> 00:58:53.450
the ones telling the stories,
to us leading the conversation.

00:58:53.450 --> 00:58:57.130
And like I said, this Pathways
and Protocols Document

00:58:57.130 --> 00:59:00.700
has been such a lifesaver
for me and I think a lot

00:59:00.700 --> 00:59:04.120
of other Indigenous creatives.

00:59:04.120 --> 00:59:06.550
I believe that we
have to wrap up--

00:59:06.550 --> 00:59:09.590
I mean, in order to ask
a couple of questions.

00:59:09.590 --> 00:59:11.830
And so I'm going to
hand it off to Shawn

00:59:11.830 --> 00:59:15.830
to provide us with
our questions.

00:59:15.830 --> 00:59:19.740
Lulu, Bird, Ligiah,
Misan, Elle-Máijá,

00:59:19.740 --> 00:59:20.990
what a beautiful conversation.

00:59:20.990 --> 00:59:23.050
And thank you for
being so transparent.

00:59:23.050 --> 00:59:25.330
The Academy Dialogues
was created as a platform

00:59:25.330 --> 00:59:27.190
to give our members
an opportunity

00:59:27.190 --> 00:59:30.070
to have truthful and
transparent conversations,

00:59:30.070 --> 00:59:31.960
and I wanted to ask
you what dialogue

00:59:31.960 --> 00:59:36.740
do you hope this conversation
will inspire in others.

00:59:36.740 --> 00:59:39.280
We can start with you, Bird.

00:59:39.280 --> 00:59:41.240
Oh, with myself?

00:59:41.240 --> 00:59:43.490
Well, I think so
many things that I

00:59:43.490 --> 00:59:49.580
feel like our panelists spoke
about today was really, about,

00:59:49.580 --> 00:59:56.060
one, the distinctness that their
voices is lending to the form--

00:59:56.060 --> 00:59:58.460
the form of storytelling,
the forms of storytelling,

00:59:58.460 --> 00:59:59.930
forms of cinema.

00:59:59.930 --> 01:00:02.930
But also, that
there are audiences

01:00:02.930 --> 01:00:04.955
who are hungry for it.

01:00:04.955 --> 01:00:06.830
That there's a disconnect
between the stories

01:00:06.830 --> 01:00:10.760
they're trying to tell and
what the audience wants.

01:00:10.760 --> 01:00:13.082
And I think that
that is our industry,

01:00:13.082 --> 01:00:14.540
and I think that
our industry could

01:00:14.540 --> 01:00:19.220
be a lot more bountiful and
successful if these voices were

01:00:19.220 --> 01:00:21.980
really supported
and really brought

01:00:21.980 --> 01:00:24.500
to the screens for
these hungry audiences

01:00:24.500 --> 01:00:29.450
to experience and to share.

01:00:29.450 --> 01:00:29.950
Thank you.

01:00:29.950 --> 01:00:32.200
Lulu, what dialogue do you
hope this conversation will

01:00:32.200 --> 01:00:34.630
inspire in others?

01:00:34.630 --> 01:00:38.890
I think that around what we were
saying of putting the pressure,

01:00:38.890 --> 01:00:43.090
putting the responsibility
on the gatekeepers

01:00:43.090 --> 01:00:48.370
themselves, the executives,
so know the writers,

01:00:48.370 --> 01:00:51.070
the directors are
not always the ones

01:00:51.070 --> 01:00:54.850
having the conversation
ourselves to say, how do we

01:00:54.850 --> 01:00:56.650
know better this situation.

01:00:56.650 --> 01:00:59.860
That hopefully, the
members will talk more

01:00:59.860 --> 01:01:03.400
and look at their own
position in the industry

01:01:03.400 --> 01:01:05.590
and their own power
and say, you know

01:01:05.590 --> 01:01:08.530
what are the questions
that I can do better.

01:01:08.530 --> 01:01:11.020
Also, how do I
better educate myself

01:01:11.020 --> 01:01:15.530
so I can participate and
be part of the solution?

01:01:15.530 --> 01:01:19.340
And Elle-Máijá,
what about yourself?

01:01:19.340 --> 01:01:22.130
Oh, wow, this has been
such a rich conversation.

01:01:22.130 --> 01:01:27.520
And I think ultimately,
I hope viewers

01:01:27.520 --> 01:01:33.280
and Academy members especially
walk away and take time

01:01:33.280 --> 01:01:36.370
to consider how
we're all implicated

01:01:36.370 --> 01:01:39.100
in this narrative, how we move
together, how we move forward

01:01:39.100 --> 01:01:40.760
together.

01:01:40.760 --> 01:01:46.270
And how can we dismantle these
toxic systems of working.

01:01:46.270 --> 01:01:49.730
And how can we build
a new way forward.

01:01:49.730 --> 01:01:54.010
How can we build models that
are rooted in equity and respect

01:01:54.010 --> 01:01:55.670
and accountability.

01:01:55.670 --> 01:01:59.420
And how can we build models
of working that are generative

01:01:59.420 --> 01:02:03.310
and consider a future
that doesn't necessarily

01:02:03.310 --> 01:02:08.290
reflect our present moment,
where we're having to have

01:02:08.290 --> 01:02:09.563
this kind of conversation.

01:02:09.563 --> 01:02:11.230
I really hope that
in 10 years from now,

01:02:11.230 --> 01:02:14.650
we're not having to have the
exact same conversations.

01:02:14.650 --> 01:02:19.270
Misan and Ligiah, you both
represent flexibility.

01:02:19.270 --> 01:02:22.160
Everyone does, but you
specifically, because Misan,

01:02:22.160 --> 01:02:23.840
you came from being
a doctor before.

01:02:23.840 --> 01:02:26.140
Ligiah, you spoke about
being an executive.

01:02:26.140 --> 01:02:28.590
For a new filmmakers
that are coming up,

01:02:28.590 --> 01:02:31.270
how important is it to
be flexible with going

01:02:31.270 --> 01:02:34.880
after your dreams
and your passions?

01:02:34.880 --> 01:02:39.040
I think from my point of view,
it's less about being flexible

01:02:39.040 --> 01:02:43.720
than about having
know confidence.

01:02:43.720 --> 01:02:45.430
What I often find--

01:02:45.430 --> 01:02:50.440
I sometimes mentor
beginning writers of color,

01:02:50.440 --> 01:02:56.230
and they say to themselves so
much, am I ready, can I do it?

01:02:56.230 --> 01:02:59.440
And we all know that
there are people

01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:01.840
from the sort of
the whiter community

01:03:01.840 --> 01:03:03.700
who've walked past a
film set and they're

01:03:03.700 --> 01:03:06.460
ready to direct a
Marvel movie, you know?

01:03:06.460 --> 01:03:09.760
Whereas as a woman and
as a woman of color,

01:03:09.760 --> 01:03:11.140
you'll say, oh, am I ready?

01:03:11.140 --> 01:03:12.940
So it's less about
being flexible

01:03:12.940 --> 01:03:14.310
than about saying, yes--

01:03:14.310 --> 01:03:16.420
you know, just having a go.

01:03:16.420 --> 01:03:21.310
And from the other side, it's
about not having everything

01:03:21.310 --> 01:03:23.970
on the shoulders of
one Black filmmaker

01:03:23.970 --> 01:03:29.250
so that if that Black filmmaker
has a go and it isn't perfect,

01:03:29.250 --> 01:03:31.000
there isn't any bigger
conversation about,

01:03:31.000 --> 01:03:32.850
oh, well, we've done our Black
film and it was a disaster,

01:03:32.850 --> 01:03:34.142
we're not going to do it again.

01:03:34.142 --> 01:03:38.140
Or making each person
representative of everything as

01:03:38.140 --> 01:03:41.120
opposed to just them just
trying to make their film.

01:03:41.120 --> 01:03:44.440
So I think that's what I hope
will people will take away

01:03:44.440 --> 01:03:48.850
from this-- that we have
sort of rich stories to tell,

01:03:48.850 --> 01:03:50.290
that we're an asset.

01:03:50.290 --> 01:03:53.320
We're not a problem to be
solved, we're an asset.

01:03:53.320 --> 01:03:57.790
And that we should
be allowed to try

01:03:57.790 --> 01:04:02.530
and allowed to participate
without the huge amount

01:04:02.530 --> 01:04:04.950
of pressure that
can be on somebody.

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:08.650
And while we should
be flexible, we

01:04:08.650 --> 01:04:18.820
should also ourselves
just not all the time

01:04:18.820 --> 01:04:21.885
or be worried about
the consequences.

01:04:21.885 --> 01:04:23.260
And those consequences
should not

01:04:23.260 --> 01:04:27.580
be disproportionate
to making a film.

01:04:27.580 --> 01:04:29.080
It works, it works,
it doesn't work.

01:04:29.080 --> 01:04:32.710
It doesn't have to be everything
so that the pressure becomes

01:04:32.710 --> 01:04:34.250
too difficult.

01:04:34.250 --> 01:04:35.000
Thank you so much.

01:04:35.000 --> 01:04:36.770
And Ligiah, did you
want to add to that?

01:04:36.770 --> 01:04:41.120
Sure, so I heard a
quote once that said

01:04:41.120 --> 01:04:47.470
a nation is only as great
as its dreams and dreams are

01:04:47.470 --> 01:04:50.420
dreamt by artists.

01:04:50.420 --> 01:04:52.550
Now I have no idea who
originally said that.

01:04:52.550 --> 01:04:58.330
I heard that from Tom
Cruise on The Actors Studio,

01:04:58.330 --> 01:05:01.460
and it has stuck with
me since that interview.

01:05:01.460 --> 01:05:06.230
So what I would say to that
new generation of filmmakers,

01:05:06.230 --> 01:05:11.170
writers, artists, is
especially in the world

01:05:11.170 --> 01:05:15.730
that we're living in, your
voices and your stories

01:05:15.730 --> 01:05:18.245
are vital to our survival.

01:05:20.830 --> 01:05:24.940
Yeah, to our survival.

01:05:24.940 --> 01:05:26.600
The second thing I
would say, though,

01:05:26.600 --> 01:05:30.850
is I heard a statistic that
less than 50% of Americans

01:05:30.850 --> 01:05:36.330
actually have a passport,
which I think is a disgrace.

01:05:36.330 --> 01:05:39.840
And even a much, much, much
lesser percentage of them

01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:42.430
speak a second language.

01:05:42.430 --> 01:05:47.880
So when you think about the most
powerful country in the world

01:05:47.880 --> 01:05:55.220
delivering the most powerful
content to the world

01:05:55.220 --> 01:06:03.210
and less than 50% of us
have actually traveled,

01:06:03.210 --> 01:06:09.530
our stories become
so much more vital.

01:06:09.530 --> 01:06:13.280
Because it is sometimes
the only window

01:06:13.280 --> 01:06:15.800
that the audience
in this country

01:06:15.800 --> 01:06:19.960
has about the outside world.

01:06:19.960 --> 01:06:23.440
And if you want to know why
this country is where it is,

01:06:23.440 --> 01:06:26.940
it's because people
don't leave this country.

01:06:26.940 --> 01:06:30.900
They're not experiencing what
is happening in the world.

01:06:30.900 --> 01:06:35.630
So just imagine how important
our stories and our point

01:06:35.630 --> 01:06:43.120
of view is in trying to
change the perception of who

01:06:43.120 --> 01:06:48.070
we are in the world
and who the world is.

01:06:48.070 --> 01:06:51.480
And that's why I think it's so
important that our stories are

01:06:51.480 --> 01:06:55.860
told and heard and considered.

01:06:55.860 --> 01:06:58.800
Wow, Ligiah, thank you so much
for that beautiful answer.

01:06:58.800 --> 01:07:01.712
Bird, Lulu, Misan,
and Elle-Máijá,

01:07:01.712 --> 01:07:03.920
thank you so much for your
time and for participating

01:07:03.920 --> 01:07:05.450
in the Academy Dialogues.

01:07:05.450 --> 01:07:10.100
And on behalf of the entire
Academy, thank you so much.

01:07:10.100 --> 01:07:11.810
Thank you for having us.

01:07:11.810 --> 01:07:14.250
Thank you, very much.

